u5# show arp        -> host 10.10.10.60.20 : NOT present
u5# show mac address-table  -> 00f0.4d00.7717 : 0 entries        (nothing reached u5)
u4# show mac address-table  -> 60  port1.0.2  00f0.4d00.7717  forward  static
   (u4 pins the MAC only as the auth subsystem's STATIC supplicant-tracking entry on the
    blocked port; with portStatus Unauthorized / packetForwarding none, no data is bridged -
    confirmed by u5 seeing nothing and every ping failing.)
  => STEP 1 PASS: every packet class - to-system-MAC, unicast, broadcast, DLF, L2 multicast -
     is dropped at the unauthenticated auth-mac port.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 - Control: authenticate the SAME MAC, verify the port now forwards
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
u4(config)# radius-server local
u4(config-radsrv)# user 00-f0-4d-00-77-17 password 00-f0-4d-00-77-17
   (host re-MAC-auths on its next frame; RADIUS now accepts the user)

u4# show auth interface port1.0.2
  portStatus: Authorized                   <-- now authenticated
  authMac: enabled

host# ping -I 10.10.60.20 10.10.60.1 (u4 system MAC)  -> 3 received, 0% packet loss
host# ping -I 10.10.60.20 10.10.60.3 (u5, across port) -> 3 received, 0% packet loss
u5# show mac address-table  -> 10/60 port2.0.24 00f0.4d00.7717 forward dynamic  (now forwarding)
u4# show radius local-server user  -> 00-f0-4d-00-77-17 present
  => STEP 2 PASS: with the identical cabling, authenticating the MAC unblocks the port and
     all traffic forwards. This confirms the Step-1 blocking was caused by auth-mac being
     unauthenticated, not by the topology.
